    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="9" relname="preparation">I agree with the comments about ease of use.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="12" relname="span">Ease of use refers to theproperty of a product or thing that a user can operate without having toovercome a steep learning curve.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">Things with high ease of use will beintuitive to the average user in the target market for the product.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="12" relname="elaboration">Theterm is often used as a goal during the design of a product, as well asbeing used for marketing purposes.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="13" relname="span">Put simply, things with "high ease ofuse" are easy to use.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="11" relname="span">However, some experts distinguish ease of use from ease of learning,</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="6" relname="circumstance">especially when the design of a product involves a tradeoff between thetwo goals.</segment>
        <group id="8" type="span"/>
        <group id="9" type="span" parent="8" relname="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="span" parent="9" relname="span"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="5" relname="concession"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="10" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="10" relname="summary"/>
    </body>
</rst>
